Date: 2026-10-30 · Checked against: latest working tree (`master`), dev server on :3001, production build `npm run build`.

## Verdict

**Ready to deploy, with the action items in the last section.** The production build passes, every internal link resolves, the conversion paths (join, contact, portal login) were exercised end to end live, and the site has clean error pages, security headers, env-held secrets, and robots.txt/sitemap.xml.

## What was fixed in this pass

| Issue | Fix |
| --- | --- |
| Join page crashed (broken `institutionGroups` import broke the whole client bundle → 500s on every public page) | [institution-picker.tsx](../src/components/forms/institution-picker.tsx) now imports `nigerianInstitutions` directly |
| Instagram reel dominated the gallery | [gallery/page.tsx](../src/app/(site)/gallery/page.tsx): compact 340 px portrait card side-by-side with its title, caption and "Open on Instagram" link on desktop; stacked on phones |
| No custom 404 | [not-found.tsx](../src/app/not-found.tsx) — branded page, verified live (HTTP 404 + copy) |
| No robots.txt | [robots.ts](../src/app/robots.ts) — allows crawlers, points at the sitemap |
| No sitemap | [sitemap.ts](../src/app/sitemap.ts) — static pages + live chapters and news posts; DB failure never blocks the build |

## Verified checks

- **Production build**: `npm run build` exits 0. Static / partial-prerender / dynamic split renders correctly; all new routes compile.
- **Internal links**: crawler walked every reachable route (35 pages, including dynamic chapter and news pages). Zero broken links, every page has an `<h1>` and a descriptive meta title.
- **Navigation**: header slots (Home, About, Chapters, Impact, Get Involved), footer columns, all footer links crawled and pass. Mobile menu verified at 400 px.
- **Forms**:
  - Contact form: submitted live, success message shown; test row deleted.
  - Join form: full path (name/email/phone → institution picker → state → category → consent) submitted live, success message shown; test row deleted from the database.
  - Portal login + media upload/save/delete verified earlier the same day against real accounts.
- **Error handling**: `(site)/error.tsx`, `portal/error.tsx` and `global-error.tsx` show calm copy and never leak stacks (details are logged server-side only).
- **Sanitization / injection**: all DB access goes through Prisma parameterized queries (the only `$queryRaw` is a tagged-template `SELECT 1` health check — safe). No `eval`, no `innerHTML`, no `document.write`; the single `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` is a static, hand-written inline script. User-supplied URLs (media, events, posts) must be `https:` before they are rendered as links/srcs — `javascript:` URLs are unreachable.
- **Secrets**: `.env*` git-ignored (template committed), no hardcoded credentials anywhere in `src/`; all `process.env` reads are server-side except the two public PostHog values (`NEXT_PUBLIC_*`).
- **Cookies/auth**: sessions use an `httpOnly` cookie; portal routes sit behind role checks.
- **Policies**: `/privacy` and `/terms` exist and are linked from every footer; the join page links the privacy policy next to the consent checkbox.
- **Metadata**: root metadata template (`%s | GLANCE`), description, OpenGraph; per-page titles confirmed by the crawl.
- **SEO files**: robots.txt + sitemap.xml live and verified.
- **Images**: `public/media` totals ~2.5 MB (largest file ≈ 200 KB); remote images limited to `res.cloudinary.com` and `i.ytimg.com`; gallery photos lazy-load; YouTube posters go through `next/image`.
- **Compression**: assets served gzip in dev; production builds emit minified, long-lived immutable `/ _next/static` bundles (platform CDN does the same). Caching of HTML is no-store in dev as expected; production relies on platform defaults.
- **Analytics**: posthog-js wired via [instrumentation-client.ts](../src/instrumentation-client.ts) with `NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_*` from env; consent tray ("Replay on / Turn off / Privacy") fires on every page; member identifies/resets are wired.
- **SSL**: HSTS (max-age 1 year) ships in production; TLS itself is provisioned by the hosting platform on the production domain.
- **Database**: `prisma migrate status` → 9 migrations, schema up to date. Fresh backup captured: `backups/glance-db-20261030-1057.dump` (custom format, 431 KB).

## Rollback plan

1. **Application rollback**: redeploy the previous release build from the platform dashboard (Vercel has instant rollback; on any other host, rebuild from the last known-good git tag: `git checkout <tag> && npm ci && npm run build && npm start`). Do not hand-edit the production `.next` directory.
2. **Data rollback**: migrations are forward-only. To restore data, load the backup into a scratch database first and verify, then swap:
   ```bash
   # 1. restore into a scratch DB (never over live data)
   pg_restore --no-owner --jobs=2 --format=custom \
     --dbname="postgresql://<scratch-user>@<host>/<scratch-db>" \
     backups/glance-db-20261030-1057.dump
   # 2. verify counts (application/member/media tables)
   # 3. point DATABASE_URL at the restored database and redeploy
   ```
   On Supabase, point-in-time restore from the dashboard is the fastest path if it is enabled on the project.
3. **Secrets**: if a key ever leaks, rotate it at the provider, update it in the platform's environment settings, and deploy — never commit real values.
4. **After any rollback**: re-run the production smoke sweep (crawl + one join submission + one contact submission) before announcing recovery.

## Remaining action items before go-live (owner)

1. Set `NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL` (and re-point `APP_ORIGIN` if used) in the production environment so robots/sitemap emit the real domain — they currently fall back to localhost.
2. Point `DATABASE_URL`/`DIRECT_URL` at the production database instance and run `npm run db:migrate` as the release step (README documents this).
3. Deploy to the chosen platform, then repeat the page crawl and one form submission against the **production** origin — staging checks do not transfer.
4. Cross-browser pass: only Chromium could be exercised in this environment. The site uses standard CSS/JS with no browser-specific APIs, but do a 15-minute manual pass in Safari, Firefox and Edge on the homepage, join form and portal login before launch.
5. Final deploy-day backup: repeat `pg_dump` right before the migration step.

