The full sweep is done. Here's everything that was checked, fixed, and added — plus what you get to do next.

## Bugs & errors: clean bill of health

- **Every route re-crawled** (all 29 sitemap URLs + 16 extra checks including the new WebP asset, 404 page, and portal routes): all return the right code, none slow.
- **Real browser pass over home, join, gallery, chapters, contact, leading-at-27**: zero JavaScript errors, zero broken images, zero failed network requests in the console.
- **Login page exercised end-to-end** in the browser: wrong-password attempt returns the clean, safe "Invalid email or password." message (no OS-independent details leaked), alert renders properly, and the form is fully wired for password managers (`autoComplete="email"` / `current-password` everywhere).
- **Concurrency of the stack**: typecheck passes (`tsc` exit 0) and the production build passes (exit 0).

## Speed fixes (this is where it was actually slow)

- **The biggest real find:** every inner page's hero background image was lazy-loading while sitting above the fold — the direct cause of slow first paint on announces/join/gallery/about/etc. Fixed with `priority` on the shared [PageHeader](src/components/site/page-header.tsx) component, which covers every page at once.
- **Heaviest assets shrunk**: the Leading-at-27 book image went **1.4 MB → 26 KB** (WebP) and the header logo lockup **349 KB → 59 KB** (WebP) — the logo renders on every single page including the portal login. References updated, and I verified both actually load and render in the browser.
- The one remaining console note is a dev-mode preload quirk on the home hero, which already has correct priority settings for production.

## Design additions (what I judged best, thought ahead)

- **Back-to-top control** on all long public pages ([back-to-top.tsx](src/components/site/back-to-top.tsx)) — disappears until you pass the first viewport, sits above the consent tray, honors reduced-motion, and I verified click → scroll-to-top → auto-hide live in the browser.
- Checked before adding anything: your foundations were already strong (focus-visible rings, `prefers-reduced-motion` coverage everywhere, theme-color, loading skeletons, search empty state, intro that plays once with skip, hash-scroll padding). So instead of piling on decoration, the polish went where it measurably helps: perceived load speed and long-page navigation.

##committed & ready

Committed as `df75b4e` — 9 files, nothing sensitive touched (`.env` still ignored, tree verified clean). **I did not push.** One command from you: `git push origin master`.

Honest limits: the "not slow" verification here is route-response timing and image/bundle weight on Chromium; true Core Web Vitals numbers (LCP/CLS seconds) need the live Vercel URL, since production serves optimized images and CDN-cached statics that dev deliberately doesn't. That check belongs in the post-deploy sweep.
