All three surfaces are fixed and verified end-to-end.

**The 3D house** — the drag was dead on touch because the browser's scroll was stealing the pointer stream; the rebuilt handler locks touch action and drives inertia that the idle spin inherits. GSAP was also overwriting the house's `translateY`, which sank it to the bottom, and the rooftop sign floated free — both are fixed by anchoring to `top` and a shared positioning formula, so "GLANCE" now sits directly on the roofline and the building stays centered on every viewport. Verified in the browser with synthetic touch pointers (rotation moved 317° → 388°) and screenshots at desktop and narrow-portrait sizes: [intro-house-desktop.png](intro-house-desktop.png), [intro-house-3.png](intro-house-3.png).

**The countdown banner** — now a slim 61px strip in the page's own flow directly above the sticky header, expanded via a smooth `max-height` transition so nothing jumps, dismissed via the same collapse. Verified at `top: 0` with the header sticking below it.

**The PostHog consent card** — [consent-banner.tsx](src/components/site/consent-banner.tsx) shrunk to a 320px compact corner card; on Allow/No-thanks it vanishes completely, no leftover pill. The choice stays reversible from a discreet "Analytics: On/Off" toggle in the footer's legal bar ([analytics-consent-toggle.tsx](src/components/site/analytics-consent-toggle.tsx)), wired to the same consent store the replay client watches. Verified the full cycle: Allow → gone, "Analytics: On" → click → denied → gone, "Analytics: Off".

`npx tsc --noEmit` passes clean, and the running dev server picked everything up.

One honest caveat: the house is still a stylized CSS-3D glass render, not a photograph of a real building — sharper and centered now, but illustrative, not photo-real. If you want it to look like a real image of an institution, that's the natural next step, and the follow-up cards above offer that alongside further intro polish and a phone-based test of the touch drag.
